## Section 2 of 4 — Data model

### `HeadlessLauncher` capability interface

New optional interface alongside `Launcher` (which stays untouched for 2.1's TTY path):

```go
// cmd/entire/cli/agent/agent.go — add next to Launcher

// HeadlessLauncher is implemented by agents that support running a prompt
// in non-interactive mode (stdout captured, process exits after response).
// This is what `entire review`'s multi-agent TUI uses to spawn N parallel
// reviews. Agents that only support interactive TTY invocation (cursor
// without --print, opencode if it has no non-interactive mode) simply
// don't implement this; they remain reachable via 2.1's single-agent path.
//
// Contract:
//   - LaunchHeadlessCmd returns an *exec.Cmd with stdout/stderr pipes
//     ready to be assigned by the caller (Stdin optional).
//   - The subprocess must terminate on its own once the prompt completes.
//   - The agent's UserPromptSubmit hook must fire during the run so the
//     pending-review marker adoption pipeline works.
type HeadlessLauncher interface {
    Agent
    LaunchHeadlessCmd(ctx context.Context, initialPrompt string) (*exec.Cmd, error)
}
```

Per-agent implementations are thin — most reuse the command shape they already use in `GenerateText` (e.g. Claude uses `claude --print`, Codex uses `codex exec`, Cursor uses `cursor-agent --print --force --trust --workspace`). No new agent-side work beyond returning an `*exec.Cmd` instead of running it directly. Agents that don't support headless simply don't implement the interface.

### `PendingReviewMarker` extension

The marker gains one field. Everything else stays backwards-compatible:

```go
type PendingReviewMarker struct {
    AgentName    string    `json:"agent_name,omitempty"`    // single-agent (Phase 1/2.1)
    AgentNames   []string  `json:"agent_names,omitempty"`   // NEW: multi-agent list
    Skills       []string  `json:"skills"`                  // unchanged
    Prompt       string    `json:"prompt,omitempty"`        // unchanged
    StartingSHA  string    `json:"starting_sha"`            // unchanged
    StartedAt    time.Time `json:"started_at"`              // unchanged
    WorktreePath string    `json:"worktree_path,omitempty"` // unchanged
}
```

**Hook adoption logic changes minimally:**

```
adoptPendingReviewMarkerInto(ctx, state, agentName):
    read marker
    if marker.AgentName != "":                       # Phase 1 path (unchanged)
        if AgentName != agentName: leave alone
        else: tag session, clear marker
    elif len(marker.AgentNames) > 0:                 # NEW multi-agent path
        if agentName not in AgentNames: leave alone
        else: tag session
              # DO NOT clear marker — orchestrator cleans up
```

No concurrent-write problem because hooks don't write back to the marker. The orchestrator owns marker lifecycle:

- Writes the marker before spawning subprocesses.
- On all-done (happy path) or on cancel / SIGTERM: `defer ClearPendingReviewMarker(ctx)` cleans it up.
- If orchestrator crashes before cleanup, the marker self-heals on next run via the existing stale-marker handling from Phase 1.

This is subtler than refcounting but way simpler operationally.

### Multi-select picker

A new helper alongside `promptForAgent`:

```go
// Returns selected agent names (1+). An empty return is an explicit user
// cancel — caller should exit silently, no marker written.
func promptForAgents(ctx context.Context, eligible []agentChoice) ([]string, error)
```

Internally uses `huh.NewMultiSelect[string]()` instead of `NewSelect`. Label format stays `"<name>   (N skills configured)"` / `"<name>   (prompt-only)"`. Default = no pre-checked options (user actively picks). Ctrl+C during picker → empty slice, `context.Canceled` error.

### `runReviewDeps` additions

Two new injection hooks for testability:

```go
type runReviewDeps struct {
    promptForAgentFn  func(ctx, []agentChoice) (string, error)       // existing (2.1)
    promptForAgentsFn func(ctx, []agentChoice) ([]string, error)     // NEW
    runMultiAgentFn   func(ctx, []MultiAgentTask) (MultiRunResult, error)  // NEW
}
```

`runMultiAgentFn` is the full orchestrator entry point. Tests stub it to simulate multi-agent runs without real subprocesses or bubbletea. Production wiring (nil → real function) kicks in when fields are unset, same pattern as 2.1.

### New types

```go
// Input to the orchestrator — one per selected agent.
type MultiAgentTask struct {
    Agent          agent.HeadlessLauncher
    Name           string               // agentChoice.Name
    Prompt         string               // composed prompt (existing composeReviewPrompt)
    TranscriptPath string               // where the agent writes its native transcript (populated after spawn via hook)
}

// Output after all agents finish or are cancelled.
type MultiRunResult struct {
    Runs []AgentRunResult  // one per input task, same order
}

type AgentRunResult struct {
    Name         string
    Status       AgentRunStatus    // Done | Failed | Cancelled
    ExitCode     int               // 0 if Done
    Duration     time.Duration
    FinalOutput  []byte            // full stdout buffer for the completion dump
    Err          error             // non-nil for Failed / Cancelled with reason
    TokenUsage   *agent.TokenUsage // nil if agent doesn't emit usable token data
}

type AgentRunStatus int
const (
    AgentRunQueued AgentRunStatus = iota
    AgentRunRunning
    AgentRunDone
    AgentRunFailed
    AgentRunCancelled
)
```

All new types live in the new file `cmd/entire/cli/review_multi.go` (orchestrator home).

Section 2 OK before I do the runtime orchestration + TUI shape (Section 3)?

