## Section 2 of 3 — Ctrl+O drill-in

### User experience

Dashboard mode (today, unchanged):
```
  ⣷ claude-code     running        28s     —
       … reviewing main.go...
  ⣷ codex           running        28s     —
       … /bin/zsh -lc 'rg --files'
```

Hit `Ctrl+O` → enter detail mode for the first agent:
```
< claude-code > (agent 1 of 2) — Esc to go back, ←/→ switch, ↑/↓ scroll
────────────────────────────────────────────
[last N lines of claude-code's stdout, live-tailing]
```

`Ctrl+O` again (or Right arrow) → cycle to next agent. `Esc` → back to dashboard. Agents keep running the whole time.

### Shared buffer type

Replace the plain `bytes.Buffer` currently used in each task's teeWriter with a small mutex-wrapped wrapper in `review_multi.go`:

```go
// agentBuffer is a thread-safe byte accumulator. The orchestrator goroutine
// writes via the io.Writer path (for stdout tee-ing); the TUI reads via
// Snapshot() on Ctrl+O drill-in. One per task.
type agentBuffer struct {
    mu  sync.Mutex
    buf bytes.Buffer
}

func (b *agentBuffer) Write(p []byte) (int, error) {
    b.mu.Lock()
    defer b.mu.Unlock()
    return b.buf.Write(p)
}

func (b *agentBuffer) Snapshot() []byte {
    b.mu.Lock()
    defer b.mu.Unlock()
    return append([]byte(nil), b.buf.Bytes()...)
}
```

Orchestrator constructs `buffers := make([]*agentBuffer, len(tasks))` before spawning goroutines. Each goroutine's teeWriter writes to its assigned `buffers[i]`. Buffers are passed to the TUI model at construction.

### TUI model additions

```go
type reviewTUIModel struct {
    // ... existing fields ...
    buffers      []*agentBuffer  // index-aligned with tasks
    termHeight   int             // tracked from tea.WindowSizeMsg (already have termWidth)
    detailMode   bool
    detailIdx    int             // which agent is in focus
    detailScroll int             // line offset from bottom; 0 = tail, positive = scrolled up
}
```

Constructor signature: `newReviewTUIModel(tasks []MultiAgentTask, onCancel func(), buffers []*agentBuffer) reviewTUIModel`.

### Update handling

```go
case tea.KeyMsg:
    if m.detailMode {
        switch msg.Type {
        case tea.KeyEsc:
            m.detailMode = false
        case tea.KeyCtrlO, tea.KeyRight:
            m.detailIdx = (m.detailIdx + 1) % len(m.tasks)
            m.detailScroll = 0
        case tea.KeyLeft:
            m.detailIdx = (m.detailIdx - 1 + len(m.tasks)) % len(m.tasks)
            m.detailScroll = 0
        case tea.KeyUp:
            m.detailScroll++
        case tea.KeyDown:
            if m.detailScroll > 0 {
                m.detailScroll--
            }
        }
    } else {
        if msg.Type == tea.KeyCtrlO && len(m.buffers) > 0 {
            m.detailMode = true
            m.detailIdx = 0
            m.detailScroll = 0
        } else if msg.Type == tea.KeyCtrlC {
            // existing cancel path (unchanged)
        }
    }
```

Note: `q` stays dashboard-only for cancel. In detail mode, only navigation keys are handled; anything else is a no-op to avoid accidentally quitting the TUI mid-read.

### View rendering (detail mode)

```go
func (m reviewTUIModel) detailView() string {
    task := m.tasks[m.detailIdx]
    data := m.buffers[m.detailIdx].Snapshot()
    lines := strings.Split(stripANSI(string(data)), "\n")

    // Viewport sizing: terminal height minus 3 (header + separator + footer)
    viewport := m.termHeight - 3
    if viewport < 5 { viewport = 5 }

    // Tail-by-default: show the last `viewport` lines, adjusted by scroll.
    end := len(lines) - m.detailScroll
    if end > len(lines) { end = len(lines) }
    start := end - viewport
    if start < 0 { start = 0 }

    var sb strings.Builder
    fmt.Fprintf(&sb, "< %s > (agent %d of %d) — Esc: back | ←/→: switch | ↑/↓: scroll\n",
        task.Name, m.detailIdx+1, len(m.tasks))
    sb.WriteString(strings.Repeat("─", 60) + "\n")
    for _, line := range lines[start:end] {
        sb.WriteString(line + "\n")
    }
    return sb.String()
}

func (m reviewTUIModel) View() string {
    if m.detailMode {
        return m.detailView()
    }
    return m.dashboardView()  // existing rendering
}
```

Detail view auto-refreshes on each tick because we already have a running ticker for the dashboard duration counter — it re-renders the whole View() which snapshots the buffer every tick.

