What the user wanted: just `codex / I'm treating this as a code review request...` and the actual review content. Everything else is structural/tool-call/log noise.

### Approach: per-agent line/block filters at dump time

Not a new file — add to `review_multi.go` alongside `dumpMultiAgentResults`.

Small type + registry:

```go
// outputFilter cleans an agent's raw stdout buffer into something suitable
// for the completion dump. Different agents have wildly different output
// verbosity; this lets each agent apply its own noise patterns.
// Nil / missing filter means the agent's output is used as-is.
type outputFilter func(raw []byte) []byte

var outputFilters = map[string]outputFilter{
    "codex":       filterCodexOutput,
    "claude-code": nil,  // --print output is already clean
    "gemini-cli":  nil,  // add when we know gemini's noise patterns
}

// applyOutputFilter looks up the per-agent filter and runs it, falling
// back to raw output when no filter exists.
func applyOutputFilter(agentName string, raw []byte) []byte {
    filter, ok := outputFilters[agentName]
    if !ok || filter == nil {
        return raw
    }
    return filter(raw)
}
```

### Codex filter

Small state machine, ~40 LOC:

```go
func filterCodexOutput(raw []byte) []byte {
    var out strings.Builder
    lines := strings.Split(string(raw), "\n")
    inExec := false
    lastBlank := false
    for _, line := range lines {
        // State: inside an exec tool-call block — skip until blank line.
        if inExec {
            if strings.TrimSpace(line) == "" {
                inExec = false
                // fall through — emit the blank for paragraph break
            } else {
                continue
            }
        }
        // Enter exec block.
        if strings.HasPrefix(line, "exec") && !strings.Contains(line, "review") {
            inExec = true
            continue
        }
        // Drop single-line noise patterns.
        if isCodexNoise(line) {
            continue
        }
        // Collapse multiple consecutive blank lines.
        blank := strings.TrimSpace(line) == ""
        if blank && lastBlank {
            continue
        }
        lastBlank = blank
        out.WriteString(line)
        out.WriteByte('\n')
    }
    return []byte(strings.TrimSpace(out.String()) + "\n")
}

var codexNoisePatterns = []*regexp.Regexp{
    regexp.MustCompile(`^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}T.+ ERROR codex_`),           // timestamped ERRORs
    regexp.MustCompile(`^hook: `),                                      // hook fire messages
    regexp.MustCompile(`^(workdir|model|provider|approval|sandbox|reasoning|session id): `),
    regexp.MustCompile(`^OpenAI Codex v`),
    regexp.MustCompile(`^-{4,}$`),                                      // separator lines
}

func isCodexNoise(line string) bool {
    for _, re := range codexNoisePatterns {
        if re.MatchString(line) {
            return true
        }
    }
    return false
}
```

Result on the example above: the dump becomes ~15 lines instead of 200, showing the agent's actual review response.

### Application point

Change one line in `dumpMultiAgentResults`:

